# 0110 WIP: keep the EP2 QMU ring populated.
#
# 0109 restored TX_SLOT(2), which made the EP2 bulk log pipe actually stream:
# 30 s produced 137 chunks with 103 distinct sequence numbers and no EPIPE.
# The remaining defect was a stable four-slot pattern: seq mod 4 = 0 and 1
# arrived once, seq mod 4 = 2 arrived twice, and seq mod 4 = 3 never arrived.
# GPD[3] stayed owned (hwo=8), and the recovery counter advanced about once
# per cycle, so the endpoint was being reset at the ring boundary.
#
# The driver armed one GPD at a time and only refilled it every 100 ms.  The
# QMU reached the next, still unarmed slot; the watchdog reset the endpoint,
# the last FIFO packet was replayed (the duplicate) and the unarmed GPD was
# skipped.  The verified P9 probe sent identical heartbeat messages, so the
# same lost slot was invisible there; a sequence-numbered stream exposed it.
#
# This version follows the vendor mtu3_qmu model on that point: one buffer
# per GPD, keep one GPD free as a sentinel, refill on TX done from the 1 ms
# thread, and use the QMU current-GPD address (TQCPR2) plus HWO to walk the
# completed GPDs like qmu_done_tx.  EP0, the 63-byte frame, TX_SLOT(2), the
# watchdog and the existing vendor status layout are unchanged.
#
# Vendor request 0xC0/0x22 is a read-only diagnostic returning TQCPR2,
# HWO, in-flight/enqueue/dequeue indices, TX-done and recovery counters.

MACOS_VERSION=13.5
KERNEL_CONFIG=DEVELOPMENT
MACHINE_CONFIG=SWEET

BASE_REPO=https://github.com/MakrSas/ios-topaz.git
BASE_SHA=5095ac19dbdcf37b64b81fe443f4027ad51b84bf
PATCHSET_SHA256=702414462b294c2d36b59981d85b4c2c07167c80f1459d2a6f3f4e2c85b02e96
BASELINE_CHANGES_SHA256=1edb66332bb4daa8b1abcd6c4de77a0e88ed9c7e9bb4a2a7045a2696e53ab338

DXB_REPO=https://github.com/blacktop/darwin-xnu-build
DXB_SHA=663906528d1cd5ea8fde7de6f16de73db9511549

KERNEL_EXPECT_EXEC_DATA=
CI_RUN_ID=
CI_KERNEL_SHA256=
CI_KERNEL_EXEC_DATA_SHA256=
CI_IMAGE_SHA256=

BOOT_CMDLINE=config/0110-boot.cmdline
